## Architecture: MVC + Service Layer

Laravel projects follow **MVC with a vertical Service Layer**:

```
app/
├── Http/
│   ├── Controllers/         # Thin controllers — no business logic here
│   ├── Requests/            # Form Request validation classes
│   └── Resources/           # API Resources for response transformation
├── Models/                  # Eloquent models (relationships, scopes, casts)
├── Services/                # Business logic layer (vertically organized)
│   ├── Auth/
│   ├── Billing/
│   ├── Notifications/
│   └── [Domain]/            # One subdirectory per domain area
├── Jobs/                    # Queue jobs for async/parallel processes
├── Policies/                # Authorization policies
├── Providers/               # Service providers
├── Exceptions/              # Custom exception classes
└── Enums/                   # PHP 8.1+ enums for domain constants
```

### Controllers: Keep Them Thin

Controllers must only:
1. Parse and validate the HTTP request (via Form Requests).
2. Call one or more Service methods.
3. Return an HTTP response.

```php
// ✅ Correct: thin controller
class PositionController extends Controller
{
    public function __construct(private readonly PositionService $positionService) {}

    public function store(StorePositionRequest $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $position = $this->positionService->create($request->validated());
        return response()->json(new PositionResource($position), 201);
    }
}

// ❌ Wrong: business logic in controller
class PositionController extends Controller
{
    public function store(Request $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $data = $request->all();
        if (!$data['title']) { return response()->json(['error' => 'Title required'], 422); }
        $position = Position::create($data);
        return response()->json($position);
    }
}
```

### Services: Business Logic Layer

- One service class per domain subdirectory.
- Services are injected via the constructor (dependency injection).
- Services call Eloquent models directly (no Repository layer needed).
- Services must not know about HTTP (no `Request`, no `Response`).

```php
// app/Services/Position/PositionService.php
class PositionService
{
    public function create(array $data): Position
    {
        $position = Position::create($data);
        // dispatch jobs, fire events, etc.
        return $position;
    }
}
```

### Form Requests: Validation

Always use Form Request classes for input validation. Never validate in controllers manually.

```php
class StorePositionRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [
            'title'       => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
            'description' => ['required', 'string'],
        ];
    }
}
```

